        ["A client rotates the head to look over the shoulder. In which plane does this motion occur?", ["Transverse plane", "Sagittal plane", "Frontal plane", "Oblique plane"], "Rotation occurs in the transverse (horizontal) plane around a vertical axis. The frontal (coronal) plane is used for side-bending and abduction; the sagittal plane for flexion and extension."],
        ["A client performs a lateral raise with dumbbells. Which plane and axis describe the shoulder motion?", ["Frontal plane, anteroposterior axis", "Sagittal plane, mediolateral axis", "Transverse plane, vertical axis", "Frontal plane, mediolateral axis"], "Abduction occurs in the frontal plane around an anteroposterior axis. Each plane pairs with the axis perpendicular to it, so a frontal plane motion cannot use a mediolateral axis."],
        ["Which tissue is characterized primarily by cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix?", ["Connective tissue", "Epithelial tissue", "Skeletal muscle tissue", "Nervous tissue"], "Connective tissue is defined by its matrix of fibers and ground substance. Epithelium consists of tightly packed cells with very little matrix between them."],
        ["A joint capsule must resist tensile loads coming from many directions. Which tissue organization BEST fits this demand?", ["Dense irregular connective tissue", "Dense regular connective tissue", "Loose areolar connective tissue", "Hyaline cartilage"], "Dense irregular tissue has collagen woven in multiple directions, ideal for multidirectional stress. Dense regular tissue is strong only along its single fiber direction, as in tendons."],
        ["During labor, uterine contractions trigger more oxytocin release, which strengthens contractions further. This loop is an example of:", ["Positive feedback", "Negative feedback", "Feedforward control", "Set point adjustment"], "The response amplifies the original stimulus until delivery ends the loop, which defines positive feedback. Negative feedback would reduce the contractions."],
        ["Six weeks after an MCL sprain, a patient feels fine and asks to return to cutting sports. What is the MOST appropriate rationale for continued graded loading?", ["Ligament collagen remodels slowly and stays weaker than native tissue for months", "Ligaments are avascular and cannot heal, so loading must stay permanently low", "Ligament tissue heals faster than muscle, so the delay is mainly about pain", "Ligament healing is complete by six weeks, so only strength needs retraining"], "Ligaments are poorly vascularized dense regular tissue; repair continues for months and tensile strength lags behind symptoms. They are not avascular, and they heal slower than muscle, not faster."]
