        ["Before applying a hot pack, you notice a reddened area over the sacrum that does not blanch when pressed. What is the best next step?", ["Protect the area from heat and pressure and report it", "Apply the hot pack to increase blood flow to the area", "Massage the area firmly to restore circulation", "Ignore it because redness usually fades within the hour"], "Non-blanchable redness over a bony prominence is a sign of early pressure injury. Adding heat or deep pressure can worsen tissue damage; protect the area and report it."],
        ["Why does a shallow abrasion heal without a scar while a deep laceration usually scars?", ["Shallow wounds resurface from intact basal cells; deep ones fill with scar", "Shallow wounds lack a blood supply, so inflammation never starts", "Deep wounds regenerate new sweat glands and hair follicles", "Deep wounds heal faster, so collagen is laid down in disorganized layers"], "When the basal layer and adnexa survive, epithelial cells resurface the wound. Deep dermal loss requires granulation tissue and fibroblast collagen, which forms scar."],
        ["Which epidermal layer is found only in the thick skin of the palms and soles?", ["Stratum lucidum", "Stratum spinosum", "Stratum basale", "Stratum granulosum"], "The stratum lucidum is a clear layer present only in thick skin. The basale, spinosum, granulosum, and corneum are present in both thick and thin skin."],
        ["A patient on bed rest has been supine for most of the day. Which sites should you inspect FIRST for pressure injury?", ["Sacrum and heels", "Ischial tuberosities and knees", "Anterior iliac crests and chin", "Lateral malleoli and greater trochanters"], "In supine, the sacrum, heels, and occiput carry the most load. Ischial tuberosities are the seated risk sites, and trochanters and malleoli are side-lying sites."],
        ["How does the epidermis receive oxygen and nutrients?", ["By diffusion from capillaries in the dermis", "From capillaries running within the stratum basale", "From blood vessels inside the stratum corneum", "Directly from the surrounding air through keratin"], "The epidermis is avascular and depends on diffusion from dermal capillaries. That is why reduced dermal perfusion, as with sustained pressure, damages the epidermis."],
        ["A client in a cold treatment room has pale, cool fingertips. Which response is the skin making?", ["Dermal vasoconstriction to conserve core heat", "Dermal vasodilation to release excess heat", "Increased sweat secretion to cool the skin", "Increased melanin to absorb more radiant heat"], "In the cold, dermal arterioles constrict, shunting blood toward the core and leaving the skin pale and cool. Vasodilation and sweating are heat-loss responses."],
        ["A patient asks why their healed deep laceration is less flexible than the surrounding skin. What is the BEST explanation?", ["Scar collagen is less organized and lacks the normal elastic network", "Scar tissue has more elastin fibers than normal dermis", "The epidermis over a scar is thicker and fully keratinized", "Scar tissue contains extra sweat glands that stiffen the area"], "Scar is fibroblast-laid collagen with disorganized alignment and little elastin, so it is less extensible than normal dermis. Adnexal structures such as glands are generally absent in scar, not increased."]
